  <p class="crumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> › <a href="/guides">Guides</a> › How to learn Mandarin: a beginner plan</p>
  <h1>How to learn Mandarin: a beginner plan</h1>
  <p class="lede">Mandarin is the most spoken language in the world, and its grammar is simpler than you might think: no verb conjugations, no plurals, no tenses. The challenge is different — tones and characters. Learn pinyin and tones first, then words, then characters slowly.</p>

  <h2>Step 1 — Learn pinyin and the four tones</h2>
  <p>Pinyin is Mandarin written with the Roman alphabet, and it is how every learner starts. The key part is tones: Mandarin has four main tones, plus a neutral tone, and the tone changes the meaning. “Ma” can mean mother (mā), hemp (má), horse (mǎ) or scold (mà), depending on the tone.</p>
  <p>Practise tones from day one, with listening and repeating. Get them right early, because tones are much harder to fix later than to learn now. Listen to the same word in all four tones until your ear can hear the difference.</p>
  <h2>Step 2 — Learn core words in pinyin first</h2>
  <p>Start with greetings (你好 — nǐ hǎo, hello; 谢谢 — xièxie, thank you; 请 — qǐng, please), numbers, time, family, food and everyday verbs. Learn words in pinyin and with their tone marks, inside short sentences: “我是学生” (wǒ shì xuéshēng — I am a student).</p>
  <p>Aim for 5–10 new words a day. Review them the next day — tones are forgotten fast if you do not review.</p>
  <h2>Step 3 — Learn characters slowly</h2>
  <p>Chinese characters look hard, but each one is built from smaller parts called radicals, and many characters tell a story: 木 (mù, tree) doubled becomes 林 (lín, forest). Start with the most common characters — 人 (person), 日 (sun/day), 月 (moon/month), 山 (mountain) — and learn each one with a word it appears in.</p>
  <p>A realistic pace is 3–5 characters a day. At that pace you will know several hundred in a few months, enough to read simple texts with help. Do not try to learn characters without pinyin and meaning together.</p>
  <h2>Step 4 — Learn Mandarin grammar (it is simple)</h2>
  <p>Mandarin grammar is famously simple: verbs never change form. “I eat”, “he eats” and “they ate” all use the same verb 吃 (chī). Time is shown with time words (昨天 — zuótiān, yesterday; 明天 — míngtiān, tomorrow) or small particles like 了 (le) for completed actions.</p>
  <p>Word order is usually subject–verb–object, like English, which makes your first sentences easy to build. The hard part is not grammar — it is tones, characters and listening.</p>
  <h2>Step 5 — Listen and speak every day</h2>
  <p>Listen to Mandarin daily — slow podcasts for learners, songs, dramas with subtitles. At first everything sounds the same, and that is normal. Your ear needs weeks to start separating tones and words.</p>
  <p>Speak from the start, even just repeating sentences. A tutor is almost essential for Mandarin, because they can hear your tones and correct them in a way no app can.</p>
  <h2>Common mistakes to avoid</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Ignoring tones — in Mandarin, the wrong tone is the wrong word.</li>
    <li>Trying to learn hundreds of characters before you can say a sentence.</li>
    <li>Learning characters without pinyin, meaning and a real word together.</li>
    <li>Only studying and never speaking.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Practical tips</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Master pinyin and tones in your first weeks — everything else builds on them.</li>
    <li>Learn 3–5 characters a day, always inside a word.</li>
    <li>Listen daily, even passively, to train your ear for tones.</li>
    <li>Speak from day one, even just repeating sentences aloud.</li>
  </ul>
