  <h2>Questions people ask</h2>

  <details><summary>How long does it take to learn Mandarin?</summary><p>With 30 minutes a day, most learners can handle simple everyday conversations in 6–12 months. Reading takes longer because of characters, but you can read simple texts within a year if you learn characters steadily. Reaching a comfortable, fluent level usually takes a few years — Mandarin is a long game, and that is normal.</p></details>
  <details><summary>Is Mandarin hard to learn?</summary><p>Mandarin has hard parts — tones and characters. But its grammar is one of the simplest of any major language: no conjugations, no plurals, no gender, no tenses. Many learners find the beginning hard and the middle smoother, once tones and basic characters click.</p></details>
  <details><summary>Do I need to learn characters, or is pinyin enough?</summary><p>Pinyin is enough to speak and to start learning, but you need characters to read anything real — menus, signs, messages, books. Start with pinyin, add characters slowly, and do not put characters off for too long.</p></details>
  <details><summary>Can I learn Mandarin by myself?</summary><p>Yes, for vocabulary, grammar and characters. Tones and speaking need a real person to listen to you and correct you — that is the part where a tutor makes the biggest difference.</p></details>
